        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0010</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0010/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference">(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>conjecture</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="area">height</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="area">frontier</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">open</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>(*): for every proper <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>-stable irreducible subvariety
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W]]></fr:tex> of a nilpotent orbit <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {O}_{\lambda }]]></fr:tex>, the isotropy bound holds —
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  W \le  \frac {1}{2} \dim  \mathcal {O}_{\lambda }]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> After <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1)</fr:link> this is the single remaining geometric
statement between the program and <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0009/" title="The height conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/" display-uri="ffi-0009" type="local">the height conjecture</fr:link>.
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">Orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link> supplies it whenever <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W]]></fr:tex>
is an orbital variety; what is left is the case of subvarieties carrying
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\ge  2]]></fr:tex> moduli of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>-orbits, where characteristic <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p]]></fr:tex> denies
the generic-smoothness argument. First possible at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 6]]></fr:tex>, which is
why the ladder <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0018/" title="Height at n ≤ 9" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0018/" display-uri="ffi-0018" type="local">height at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  9]]></fr:tex></fr:link> has to be climbed by
hand.</html:p>
            <html:p>The infinitesimal form of the same statement is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">Orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex Part 3; journal sec:h1 and the pass 6--8
apparatus.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0052/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0052</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0052/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="(L2): decreasing monomial germs are vacuous">(L2): decreasing monomial germs are vacuous</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">frontier</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="avatar">3</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>(L2). Decreasing monomial germs are vacuous in every
characteristic.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> One of the two positive results the germ-scale search produced.
Together with the witness principle — a single component-generic germ
certifies its whole component — it is what remains standing after
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0053/" title="(L3): the vacuity family" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0053/" display-uri="ffi-0053" type="local">(L3)</fr:link> and <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link> fell.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0049/" title="The germ apparatus" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0049/" display-uri="ffi-0049" type="local">The germ apparatus</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: journal passes 8--10 (shift calculus, monomial census);
scratch_L2.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0053/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0053</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0053/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="(L3): the vacuity family">(L3): the vacuity family</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>conjecture</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">frontier</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="avatar">3</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">refuted</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>(L3), in all three of its forms — (L3), (L3<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[']]></fr:tex>), (L3<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA['']]></fr:tex>) —
asserted that the relevant germ components are vacuous.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Status.</html:strong> <html:strong>Refuted.</html:strong> Nonvacuous components exist from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 4]]></fr:tex>
onward, and the component atlas of the tenth pass exhibits them
explicitly at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 9]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 10]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Consequence.</html:strong> Refuting the family is what forced the wall program: with
nonvacuous components in hand, the question became what happens <html:em>at</html:em>
them, which is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>. The eleventh-pass two-generator
lattice normal form was the attack route; the depth law for
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lambda _2 \ge  3]]></fr:tex> is still open, and no closed form exists for
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r \ge  3]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Superseded by:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W): the wall conjecture</fr:link>, and after its refutation, the stable depth
law.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0049/" title="The germ apparatus" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0049/" display-uri="ffi-0049" type="local">The germ apparatus</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0052/" title="(L2): decreasing monomial germs are vacuous" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0052/" display-uri="ffi-0052" type="local">(L2): decreasing monomial germs are vacuous</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: journal passes 8--11; scratch_L3, scratch_sweep,
scratch_tenth, scratch_eleventh.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0049/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0049</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0049/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The germ apparatus">The germ apparatus</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="avatar">3</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>A <html:em>pencil germ</html:em> is a lattice over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B = k\lbrack \lbrack  u \rbrack \rbrack \lbrack  u^{-1} \rbrack ]]></fr:tex>, a
polynomial-type ring in one variable over the formal power series ring
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[R = k\lbrack \lbrack  u \rbrack \rbrack ]]></fr:tex>. The <html:em>survivor algebra</html:em> of a germ carries
a cyclic cocycle; that cocycle is the germ-scale avatar of the trace
form.</html:p>
            <html:p>The <html:em>exponent dictionary</html:em> computes its graded pieces: the
dimension of the level-<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[m]]></fr:tex> piece is <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  r + \#\{\text {adjoint-torsion exponents of size} \ge  m\},
]]></fr:tex>
a consequence of the isomorphism <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {C}_{P} \cong  \mathcal {O}]]></fr:tex>
between the centralizer sheaf of the germ's pencil <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> and the
structure sheaf.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> This is avatar 3 — <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0013/" title="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/" display-uri="ffi-0013" type="local">the thesis</fr:link> made infinitesimal.
Avatar 2, the residue form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {tr}([P, A]B)]]></fr:tex>, has its <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[u]]></fr:tex>-adic
bottom here. Coisotropy of the cocycle is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0017/" title="Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0017/" display-uri="ffi-0017" type="local">Wall 1</fr:link>; the
attempt to upgrade it to a Lagrangian statement is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0013/" title="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/" display-uri="ffi-0013" type="local">The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex Part 5; journal passes 8--11 (exponent
dictionary, component atlas).</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0013</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0013/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension">The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>principle</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Every bound the program needs is half of a nilpotent orbit dimension.
Nilpotent orbits carry the Kostant--Kirillov form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex> in every
characteristic; a subvariety of half dimension inside an orbit sits at
the Lagrangian bound, and dimension <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\le ]]></fr:tex> half is exactly what isotropy
forces.</html:p>
            <html:p>The thesis appears at three scales, and the three avatars of the
forest are its three instances.</html:p>
            <html:ul><html:li><html:strong>Global</html:strong> — the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex> form on each orbit:
  <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link>, a theorem.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Semi-local</html:strong> — the residue form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {tr}([P, A]B)]]></fr:tex> on
  adjoint torsion at drop points: <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1)</fr:link>, a theorem.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Infinitesimal</html:strong> — the survivor cocycle at a pencil germ:
  <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>, a conjecture.</html:li></html:ul>
            <html:p>This is a principle, not a theorem: it is the reason the three
avatars have the same shape, and it is what tells you that
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link> is the right thing to be stuck on.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0012/" title="The three recurring numbers" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0012/" display-uri="ffi-0012" type="local">The three recurring numbers</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex Part 2 (the avatar structure).</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0017/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0017</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0017/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle">Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="avatar">3</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Wall 1. The cyclic cocycle carried by the survivor algebra of a
pencil germ is coisotropic.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> This is the bottom floor of the avatar tower: the germ-scale
instance of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0013/" title="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/" display-uri="ffi-0013" type="local">the thesis</fr:link>, and the fixed point from which
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link> tries to upgrade coisotropy to the full Lagrangian
statement. It is a theorem; the upgrade is not.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0049/" title="The germ apparatus" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0049/" display-uri="ffi-0049" type="local">The germ apparatus</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: journal pass 6--8 apparatus (Borel/Steinberg, Wall 1);
distilled.tex Part 5.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/sec-frontier/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>sec-frontier</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/sec-frontier/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="finite-field-invariants: a reference forest › The frontier"><fr:link href="/ffi/index/" title="finite-field-invariants: a reference forest" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/index/" display-uri="index" type="local">finite-field-invariants: a reference forest</fr:link> › The frontier</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Two live fronts. The (NS) chain is the sole residue of the growth
  program; the germ-scale front is where the wall program ran out, and
  is kept on the record because what it refuted is load-bearing.</html:p>
            <html:ul><html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0042/" title="Splits, flanks, tallness" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0042/" display-uri="ffi-0042" type="local">Splits and tallness</fr:link> — splits, flanks, tallness.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0043/" title="Lemma V" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0043/" display-uri="ffi-0043" type="local">Lemma V</fr:link> — false as stated.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0044/" title="Tall-Lemma V" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0044/" display-uri="ffi-0044" type="local">Tall-Lemma V</fr:link> — true where it matters.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0045/" title="(NS): the strict/centralizer inequality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0045/" display-uri="ffi-0045" type="local">(NS)</fr:link> — the open inequality.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0046/" title="The Fredholm ledger" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0046/" display-uri="ffi-0046" type="local">The Fredholm ledger</fr:link> — the exact ledger.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0047/" title="(Q-MOD): the reductive repair" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0047/" display-uri="ffi-0047" type="local">(Q-MOD)</fr:link> — the first reduction.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0048/" title="The peeling induction" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0048/" display-uri="ffi-0048" type="local">The peeling induction</fr:link> — the second reduction.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link> — refuted from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 6]]></fr:tex>.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0052/" title="(L2): decreasing monomial germs are vacuous" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0052/" display-uri="ffi-0052" type="local">(L2)</fr:link> — proven.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0053/" title="(L3): the vacuity family" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0053/" display-uri="ffi-0053" type="local">(L3)</fr:link> — refuted, and productive.</html:li></html:ul>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false" hidden-when-empty="true">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:title text="Related">Related</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0010</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0010/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference">(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>conjecture</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="area">height</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="area">frontier</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">open</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>(*): for every proper <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>-stable irreducible subvariety
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W]]></fr:tex> of a nilpotent orbit <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {O}_{\lambda }]]></fr:tex>, the isotropy bound holds —
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  W \le  \frac {1}{2} \dim  \mathcal {O}_{\lambda }]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> After <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1)</fr:link> this is the single remaining geometric
statement between the program and <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0009/" title="The height conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/" display-uri="ffi-0009" type="local">the height conjecture</fr:link>.
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">Orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link> supplies it whenever <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W]]></fr:tex>
is an orbital variety; what is left is the case of subvarieties carrying
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\ge  2]]></fr:tex> moduli of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>-orbits, where characteristic <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p]]></fr:tex> denies
the generic-smoothness argument. First possible at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 6]]></fr:tex>, which is
why the ladder <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0018/" title="Height at n ≤ 9" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0018/" display-uri="ffi-0018" type="local">height at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  9]]></fr:tex></fr:link> has to be climbed by
hand.</html:p>
            <html:p>The infinitesimal form of the same statement is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">Orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex Part 3; journal sec:h1 and the pass 6--8
apparatus.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0049/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0049</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0049/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The germ apparatus">The germ apparatus</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="avatar">3</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>A <html:em>pencil germ</html:em> is a lattice over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B = k\lbrack \lbrack  u \rbrack \rbrack \lbrack  u^{-1} \rbrack ]]></fr:tex>, a
polynomial-type ring in one variable over the formal power series ring
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[R = k\lbrack \lbrack  u \rbrack \rbrack ]]></fr:tex>. The <html:em>survivor algebra</html:em> of a germ carries
a cyclic cocycle; that cocycle is the germ-scale avatar of the trace
form.</html:p>
            <html:p>The <html:em>exponent dictionary</html:em> computes its graded pieces: the
dimension of the level-<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[m]]></fr:tex> piece is <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  r + \#\{\text {adjoint-torsion exponents of size} \ge  m\},
]]></fr:tex>
a consequence of the isomorphism <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {C}_{P} \cong  \mathcal {O}]]></fr:tex>
between the centralizer sheaf of the germ's pencil <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> and the
structure sheaf.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> This is avatar 3 — <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0013/" title="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/" display-uri="ffi-0013" type="local">the thesis</fr:link> made infinitesimal.
Avatar 2, the residue form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {tr}([P, A]B)]]></fr:tex>, has its <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[u]]></fr:tex>-adic
bottom here. Coisotropy of the cocycle is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0017/" title="Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0017/" display-uri="ffi-0017" type="local">Wall 1</fr:link>; the
attempt to upgrade it to a Lagrangian statement is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0013/" title="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/" display-uri="ffi-0013" type="local">The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex Part 5; journal passes 8--11 (exponent
dictionary, component atlas).</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0013</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0013/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension">The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>principle</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Every bound the program needs is half of a nilpotent orbit dimension.
Nilpotent orbits carry the Kostant--Kirillov form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex> in every
characteristic; a subvariety of half dimension inside an orbit sits at
the Lagrangian bound, and dimension <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\le ]]></fr:tex> half is exactly what isotropy
forces.</html:p>
            <html:p>The thesis appears at three scales, and the three avatars of the
forest are its three instances.</html:p>
            <html:ul><html:li><html:strong>Global</html:strong> — the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\omega ]]></fr:tex> form on each orbit:
  <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link>, a theorem.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Semi-local</html:strong> — the residue form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {tr}([P, A]B)]]></fr:tex> on
  adjoint torsion at drop points: <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1)</fr:link>, a theorem.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:strong>Infinitesimal</html:strong> — the survivor cocycle at a pencil germ:
  <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>, a conjecture.</html:li></html:ul>
            <html:p>This is a principle, not a theorem: it is the reason the three
avatars have the same shape, and it is what tells you that
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link> is the right thing to be stuck on.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0012/" title="The three recurring numbers" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0012/" display-uri="ffi-0012" type="local">The three recurring numbers</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: distilled.tex Part 2 (the avatar structure).</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0017/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0017</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0017/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle">Wall 1: coisotropy of the cyclic cocycle</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">symplectic</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="avatar">3</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Wall 1. The cyclic cocycle carried by the survivor algebra of a
pencil germ is coisotropic.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> This is the bottom floor of the avatar tower: the germ-scale
instance of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0013/" title="The thesis: every bound is half an orbit dimension" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0013/" display-uri="ffi-0013" type="local">the thesis</fr:link>, and the fixed point from which
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link> tries to upgrade coisotropy to the full Lagrangian
statement. It is a theorem; the upgrade is not.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">The trace form</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0049/" title="The germ apparatus" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0049/" display-uri="ffi-0049" type="local">The germ apparatus</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: journal pass 6--8 apparatus (Borel/Steinberg, Wall 1);
distilled.tex Part 5.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
